<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#duke-of-aveiro">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke of Aveiro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1535">1535</a>–<a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as House of Aveiro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke of Aveiro was a Portuguese ducal title created in 1535 for João de Lencastre, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II. The dukes were among the richest nobles of Portugal and held lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, including the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Seguro. The title was extinguished when the 8th duke was executed in 1759 for alleged involvement in an attempt on the life of King Joseph I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-of-aveiro.jpg" alt="Duke of Aveiro" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RicardoFilipePereira · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padr%C3%A3o_do_Ch%C3%A3o_Salgado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
